@@ -51,6 +51,8 @@ The coding overhead required for megayear integrity is substantial but affordabl
 
 Which error types the code must carry is less open than the substitution-first framing suggests. A high-dimensional mediation analysis of an aging experiment on electrochemically synthesized DNA finds that every one of the 14 damage components significantly linking sequence composition to storage failure is a single-base deletion, with the mediated effects concentrated in trinucleotide contexts ending in C (Li, Wu & Zhang 2026). Two requirements follow for the archive: the code must be indel-correcting rather than substitution-only, and the encoder must treat local sequence context, not gross GC fraction alone, as a constraint on which strands it is permitted to emit. One aging experiment on one synthesis chemistry fixes the direction of that requirement, not its magnitude.
 
+Constraint-aware encoding is now demonstrated at system scale rather than argued in principle. Wang et al. (2026) encode in linear time while proactively excluding homopolymer runs, tandem repeats, and candidate promoter motifs, pair that with a two-tiered index so that stored units remain individually addressable, and recover their payloads with a 100% success rate across their in vitro and in vivo tests; the addressable design space they report, above 270 PB, is a theoretical figure rather than a demonstrated one. The ordering is what matters for the archive: the binding limit on recovered bits per gram is the encoder's constraint budget and its index, not the raw 2 bits/nt of the four-letter alphabet, so the constraints belong in the encoder rather than in a downstream repair stage.
