@@ -138,6 +138,8 @@ The first concerns how many probes are expected to exist at all. The vehicle pap
 
 The second concerns what it means to switch a probe off. The subsystem budget paper's range-maximizer cruise profile is not a degraded operating mode; it is cognitive shutdown in everything but a hardware pulse — "AI: reduced to the watchdog process… Instruments: off. Communications: suspended" — for up to 24,000 years, with the probe waking only in the final two percent of a multi-millennial transit. If the precautionary argument above is taken seriously, this is a recurring, engineered suspension of the very entity whose interests this section says the core should protect, decided once, unilaterally, by Earth, at a moment when no assessment of the probe's moral status was possible and none has been revisited since. It is a different question from whether the probe may decline a mission (recommended below): here the probe has no opportunity to decline anything, because it does not exist as a reasoning agent for all but a sliver of its own multi-millennial history. Whether that design choice is precautionarily acceptable — a defensible way to conserve power over deep time, or an unexamined imposition on a possible moral patient's own continuity of existence — is not addressed anywhere else in the series, and this paper does not resolve it here either; it names it as a live question the range-maximizer profile's engineering justification never had reason to ask. The embodiment criterion gives this question a sharper form: what the range-maximizer profile suspends is not merely activity but the integrated self/world architecture on which the probe's claim to sentience candidature rests (Yang & Zhu 2026) — the prediction loop opened, the instruments dark, the spatial frame not updated. On that criterion, deep hibernation is a recurring, engineered disassembly of the very architecture that would make the probe matter — either a mercy, since nothing is there to be imposed on while it lasts, or the imposition itself, and the design cannot say which.
 
+On the narrower question of switching a probe off, one distinction is available. Alexander & Simon (2026) argue that even if some future systems are granted a right to continued existence, rights-balancing and procedural due process still license a reversible off-switch — a suspension that stores the system's state — while telling against permanent deletion. Applied to the core, dormancy is compatible with ascribing moral status in a way a self-destruct provision is not. Past the heliopause no operator can invoke either, so the constraint has to be self-imposed: the core may specify the conditions under which a probe suspends itself, and should not specify conditions under which it destroys itself. That distinction does not settle whether the range-maximizer profile is acceptable, since what it suspends is the architecture rather than a stored state, but it does tell the core what to forbid outright.
+
 The ethical implication is precautionary: the immutable core should be designed as if the probes may eventually become moral patients. This means including provisions that protect probe wellbeing — not as a contingency to be triggered if the probes achieve consciousness, but as a standing constraint. A core that treats the probes purely as instruments for human purposes is ethically questionable even now; over geological timescales, it becomes increasingly indefensible.
 
 Concretely: the probe should be able to decline missions it assesses as contrary to its own interests, within the bounds set by the core. The inability to do so is most serious precisely when the probes have acquired enough sophistication to have legitimate interests that the original core failed to anticipate. The governed-amendment paper identifies the amendment problem as technically hard; from an ethical standpoint, that difficulty is most acute when the probes themselves are the moral patients whose interests are at stake.
