@@ -128,6 +128,8 @@ The first is the measure itself. Reachability here is binary: a node is counted
 
 The second is the attack itself. `targeted_removal()` selects greedily, and greedy selection is myopic: optimal k-node targeting can only do better. Both limitations point the same way, so the three-to-four-fold figure should be read as a conservative bound on adversary effectiveness rather than as a point estimate.
 
+How much better is partly measurable. Ramrakhiyani (2026) compares non-adaptive, semi-adaptive and fully adaptive node removal and reports that dispersion-oriented targeting matches or exceeds degree-based dismantling at shrinking the largest connected component, with the margin widest where the attacker recomputes its target list after each removal, and that the semi-adaptive variant recovers most of the adaptive gain at a fraction of the recomputation cost. Articulation points as such are not among the strategies tested, so this transfers as a direction and not as a number. It runs the same way as the two limitations above: re-planning is cheap enough to sit inside an adversary's compute budget, which makes the fixed-list assumption behind the leverage curve the conservative one.
+
 **The root.** The random-removal analysis excludes the Sun, and the targeted analysis above inherits the exclusion. The roadmap paper gives the exclusion a cost. Under its settlement-zero architecture the home-system node is not Earth but a main-belt settlement that dispatches the entire first generation — a dozen or more seeds, on that paper's argument and the routing paper's root-degree runs — before any other node exists. For the first several thousand years settlement zero is therefore the articulation point of the whole network: every route passes through it; its loss before the first seeds leave is the lineage's extinction, and its loss before the first children settle is the loss of every further first-generation dispatch and of the root-degree advantage the roadmap and routing papers argue for. The threat that reaches it is also one this paper's adversary model does not contain. Sections 5 and 6 assume an adversary that is external, another lineage or an infiltrator, and that must first get in. Settlement zero's likeliest adversary is already in: the roadmap paper names capture — the diversion of a belt settlement with unprecedented industrial capacity to some purpose other than launching seeds, by the civilization that built it — as a failure mode requiring no hostile lineage at all. Containment does nothing against it, because there is no lineage yet within which to bound a blast radius; the counselor posture does not apply, because the party is not an infiltrator but the owner. The only defense available is the ethics paper's: the constraints on the immutable core must be in place, and the settlement's governance must be defensible and contestable, before the settlement has anything worth capturing. Once the first children settle, the ordinary argument of this paper resumes — the root becomes one node among several, its loss bounded, and security relocates to the lineage. The window in which the root is the whole lineage is the window in which this paper's architecture provides no protection, and the roadmap paper's on-ramp is where that window is either closed or left open.
 
 Two design consequences follow, and one of them is already in the series.
